---
.title = "How to build a full IDE in Neovim from scratch (0.12)",
.date = .date("2026-08-22T00:00:00"),
.layout = "post.shtml",
---

Hello! In this tutorial we will go over how to create a modern IDE inside of Neovim from scratch. You will learn how to structure your configuration, install and configure plugins, and set up core parts of an IDE such as fuzzy file finding and code completion. I've created a [tutorial repository](https://github.com/trevorhauter/neovim-2026-tutorial) you can use as reference while you follow along. Let's jump right in!

First things first, let's [install Neovim](https://neovim.io/doc/install/). Once you've done that, you can launch neovim inside a terminal with the `nvim` command.

Next, let's create a directory to store our configuration. 

```sh
mkdir ~/.config/nvim
```

This is where neovim will look every time it's launched. I recommend storing this in version control, either with your other dotfiles or in a repository on its own. To prevent overloading of the term "configuration", I'm going to refer to the files inside the `~/.config/nvim` directory as our *project* going forward.

Let's establish our core project structure. This will keep things nice and organized as we expand/modify our project!

We're going to create a directory for our core configuration options (native nvim settings), another for plugins, and an init file to tell Neovim where to look when everything is loaded up. We can create all of these by running the following commands inside our root project directory `~/.config/nvim`

```sh
touch init.lua && mkdir lua \
&& mkdir lua/config && touch lua/config/options.lua \
&& mkdir lua/plugins && touch lua/plugins/init.lua
```

Which yields the following tree structure

```
~/.config/nvim
├── init.lua
└── lua
    ├── config
    │   └── options.lua
    └── plugins
        └── init.lua
```

Now, when neovim launches, it will look in the `init.lua` file at our project root. Let's point the `init.lua` file to our `lua/config/options.lua`, which is going to be the central file that loads everything else we care about.

```lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua
require("config.options")
```

Now, let's add some of our first settings into our `lua/config/options.lua` file!

```lua
-- /lua/config/options.lua
vim.g.mapleader = " " -- Prefix used for many keybinds

require("plugins") -- Will load plugins directory automatically

vim.diagnostic.config({ virtual_text = true }) -- Enables lsp warnings

-- Personal preferences!
vim.bo.shiftwidth = 2
vim.bo.softtabstop = 2
vim.bo.tabstop = 2

vim.o.winborder = "rounded"

vim.opt.expandtab = true
vim.opt.hlsearch = false
vim.opt.nu = true
vim.opt.relativenumber = true
vim.opt.scrolloff = 8
vim.opt.signcolumn = "yes"
vim.opt.smartindent = true
vim.opt.swapfile = false
vim.opt.wrap = true
```

This will get us started with some good defaults. If you aren't sure what a setting does and would like to know, simply type `:h $setting` inside Neovim to open up the documentation! Or use a search engine to learn more. There's no right settings to have here, this is just where I've landed personally, and is a fine starting point.

Next, in our `lua/plugins/init.lua` file, let's add some simple code so that when new plugins are added, Neovim loads them in automatically. This will make managing our project a lot easier! :-)

```lua
-- lua/plugins/init.lua
-- Require all files in directory

local config_path = vim.fn.stdpath("config")
local directory_name = "plugins"

for _, file in
    ipairs(
        vim.fn.readdir(
            config_path .. "/lua/" .. directory_name,
            [[v:val =~ '\.lua$']]
        )
    )
do
    local file = file:gsub("%.lua$", "")

    if file ~= "init" then
        require(directory_name .. "." .. file)
    end
end
```

Now, when we want to add new plugins, this code will find them and let Neovim know, so we don't have to worry about requiring everything manually ourselves. How fun! At this point, [your project should look like this](https://github.com/trevorhauter/neovim-2026-tutorial/tree/7c2bac2d147a6ec4b918efaf4b9e71ee3e5907cd).

Now we're off to the races! First thing I like to do is select a theme. Neovim is just as much about form as it is function! My favorite theme is [gruvbox-material](https://github.com/sainnhe/gruvbox-material), but you can choose any you like.

